win32u: SetFocus() on another thread's top-level moves the foreground with the activation

Premiere Pro: click away to another app, click back, and about a second later the app gains
focus, loses it, and regains it. Independent of the file dialog (reproduces on the home screen
with no dialog present) and of which app you click away to. Operator: "this is a legitimate
focus, unfocus, refocus process when clicking back on the app."

Captured 2026-09-16 (premiere-20260916-185237.log, WINEDEBUG=+win,+waylanddrv, 11.10-90, no
dialog). Every cycle, a freshly spawned CEF process (wayland_process_init on its first thread)
creates a WS_CHILD|WS_VISIBLE "CefBrowserWindow" inside a zero-sized CEF host top-level owned by
Premiere's main thread, and runs CEF's focus sequence on the child:

    0a10 set_foreground_window hwnd 0x2017a, mouse 0, internal 0 -> ret 0     <- denied, correctly
    0a10 NtUserSetFocus 0x2017a prev 0x1015c
    0a10 set_active_window hwnd 0x30146, previous 0x10156, mouse 0, focus 0   <- active moves alone
    0178 set_foreground_window hwnd 0x30146, previous 0x30146 -> ret 1        <- foreground, LATE
    0178 set_foreground_window hwnd 0x10156, previous 0x30146 -> ret 1        <- and unwinds again

0x2017a is a child, so the WS_CHILD walk above resolves hwndTop to the host 0x30146, which
belongs to thread 0178, not to the caller 0a10. Four foreground transitions per cycle where
Windows has two, and in between Wine sits in a state Windows never enters: an active window that
is not the foreground window. That desynchronised pair, not any repaint, is what the operator
sees as gain/lose/regain.

Windows (HANDOFF-61, Win11 26200, measured with a control program owning all the windows --
rc-scripts/ncact_child.py T6, a second process parenting a WS_CHILD|WS_VISIBLE into a hidden
0x86000000 popup and running SetWindowPos/SetForegroundWindow/SetFocus, exactly CEF's sequence):

    [child proc] SetForegroundWindow(child) -> 0 err=0        <- Windows denies it too
    [child proc] SetFocus(child) -> prev=1838536
    STATE T6   fg=B active=B focus=B | ACTIVECAPTION: A=0 B=1

So the denial is not the difference -- Windows denies the same call -- and neither is the steal:
Windows performs it. The difference is that on Windows SetFocus moves the foreground, the active
window and the focus together, in one transition, even though the caller's own
SetForegroundWindow was just refused. Wine reaches set_foreground_window() from NtUserSetFocus
only when there is no active window at all:

    if (!(active = get_active_window()) && !set_foreground_window( hwndTop, FALSE, FALSE )) return 0;
    if (hwndTop != active)
        if (!set_active_window( hwndTop, NULL, FALSE, FALSE, 0 )) return 0;

Here active is 0x10156, so the short circuit skips the foreground move and only the active window
changes; the owning thread's own SetForegroundWindow then catches up as a separate transition and
has to be unwound the same way.

Fix: in NtUserSetFocus, when the resolved top-level is not already active, belongs to a different
thread than the caller, and is not already the foreground window, move the foreground to it
(internal, as Windows does after refusing the application's explicit call) instead of moving the
active window alone. set_foreground_window() performs the activation itself, so the pair stays in
step and the cycle is one transition each way. The same-thread case -- a thread activating its own
top-level, the common path for every application -- is untouched, as is the active == 0 branch and
every other set_active_window() caller.

Kill switch NEUTRON_SETFOCUS_FOREGROUND=0 restores the upstream behaviour without a rebuild;
NEUTRON_WL_DIAG=1 prints one neutron-setfocus-fg line per move, at the outcome.

Supersedes an earlier attempt of the same file (hidden cross-thread targets took no activation at
all), which the Windows measurement above refuted: the activation is correct, only its pairing
with the foreground was wrong.

--- a/dlls/win32u/input.c
+++ b/dlls/win32u/input.c
@@ -2217,6 +2217,51 @@
 /*****************************************************************
  *           NtUserSetFocus  (win32u.@)
  */
+
+/* NEUTRON: SetFocus() on a top-level owned by another thread.
+ *
+ * Windows moves the foreground window, the active window and the focus together here, in one
+ * transition, even when the caller's own SetForegroundWindow() was refused a moment earlier
+ * (measured on Win11 26200 with a control program owning every window: a second process parents
+ * a WS_CHILD|WS_VISIBLE into a hidden 0x86000000 popup and runs CEF's
+ * SetWindowPos/SetForegroundWindow/SetFocus sequence; SetForegroundWindow returns 0 and SetFocus
+ * then leaves fg == active == focus == that popup -- HANDOFF-61 T6).
+ *
+ * Wine could only reach set_foreground_window() from here when no window was active at all, so
+ * with an active window present it moved the active window alone and left the foreground behind.
+ * The owning thread's own SetForegroundWindow then arrived as a second transition and was unwound
+ * as a third and fourth, and in between the active window was not the foreground window -- a
+ * state Windows does not enter. Premiere's CEF hosts do this on every click-back, one per spawned
+ * CEF process, and the application sees activate/deactivate/activate.
+ *
+ * Only the divergent branch is touched: the target must be a top-level owned by a different
+ * thread that is neither already active nor already the foreground window. A thread activating
+ * its own top-level -- the common path -- keeps the upstream behaviour.
+ *
+ * Kill switch NEUTRON_SETFOCUS_FOREGROUND=0 restores the upstream behaviour without a rebuild;
+ * NEUTRON_WL_DIAG=1 reports each move, at the outcome. */
+static BOOL neutron_setfocus_foreground_on(void)
+{
+    static int on = -1;
+
+    if (on < 0)
+    {
+        const char *e = getenv( "NEUTRON_SETFOCUS_FOREGROUND" );
+        on = e ? (e[0] != '0') : 1;
+        ERR( "neutron-gateresolved: NEUTRON_SETFOCUS_FOREGROUND = %d (%s)\n", on, e ? "environment" : "default" );
+    }
+    return on;
+}
+
+static BOOL neutron_setfocus_moves_foreground( HWND top, HWND active )
+{
+    if (!top || !active) return FALSE;              /* the active == 0 branch already foregrounds */
+    if (!neutron_setfocus_foreground_on()) return FALSE;
+    if (get_window_thread( top, NULL ) == GetCurrentThreadId()) return FALSE;
+    if (top == NtUserGetForegroundWindow()) return FALSE;   /* nothing to move */
+    return TRUE;
+}
+
 HWND WINAPI NtUserSetFocus( HWND hwnd )
 {
     HWND hwndTop = hwnd, active;
@@ -2257,7 +2302,15 @@
         if (!(active = get_active_window()) && !set_foreground_window( hwndTop, FALSE, FALSE )) return 0;
         if (hwndTop != active)
         {
-            if (!set_active_window( hwndTop, NULL, FALSE, FALSE, 0 )) return 0;
+            if (neutron_setfocus_moves_foreground( hwndTop, active ))
+            {
+                /* set_foreground_window() activates hwndTop itself, keeping the pair in step */
+                if (!set_foreground_window( hwndTop, FALSE, TRUE )) return 0;
+                if (neutron_wl_diag())
+                    ERR( "neutron-setfocus-fg: hwnd=%p top=%p (thread %04x, not ours) -> moved the foreground with the activation, was active %p\n",
+                         hwnd, hwndTop, (unsigned int)get_window_thread( hwndTop, NULL ), active );
+            }
+            else if (!set_active_window( hwndTop, NULL, FALSE, FALSE, 0 )) return 0;
             if (!is_window( hwnd )) return 0;  /* Abort if window destroyed */
 
             /* Do not change focus if the window is no longer active */
